<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-arginine degradation X (arginine monooxygenase pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="ARGDEG-V-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Several Actinobacteria, including |FRAME: TAX-1911|, |FRAME: TAX-67377|, |FRAME: TAX-1704| and |FRAME: TAX-1665| can utilize |FRAME: ARG| as the sole source for carbon and nitrogen utilizing a pathway that involves the intermediate |FRAME:4-GUANIDO-BUTYRAMIDE| |CITS: [Thoai62b][Thoai62a][Yorifuji89][7766193]|. The same pathway was also observed in the eukaryotic parasite |FRAME: TAX-5661| |CITS: [3600696]|. In this pathway |FRAME: ARG| is degraded via |FRAME:4-GUANIDO-BUTYRAMIDE|, |FRAME: CPD-592|, and |FRAME: 4-AMINO-BUTYRATE|, which is eventually degraded via |FRAME: SUCC-S-ALD| into |FRAME: SUC|, and fed into the |FRAME: TCA| (see the pathway |FRAME: 4AMINOBUTMETAB-PWY|) |CITS: [13373855][5936244]|. |FRAME: AMMONIA| and |FRAME: UREA| are produced during this process, providing the direct nitrogen source for the organism (urea can be used after it is split by the enzyme |FRAME: CPLX-6897| into |FRAME: AMMONIA| and |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE|). The enzymes involved, |FRAME: EC-1.13.12.1|, |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.4|, and |FRAME: EC-3.5.3.7|, were shown to be induced by |FRAME: ARG| added to the culture medium. A commercial process has been developed to produce |FRAME: CPLX-85406| from |FRAME: TAX-1911| in 20 and 200 liter tank fermentors |CITS: [Delcambe65]|. There is also evidence that this pathway can occur in citrus trees (|FRAME: TAX-55188|) |CITS: [Kato86]|, and the intermediate compounds have been found in several other plant species |CITS: [Kato86a]|. In some |FRAME: TAX-1883| species the intermediate |FRAME: CPD-592| serves as a starter unit for the biosynthesis of several guanidine-containing polyketide products , such as |FRAME: CPD-25992| and desulfoclethramycin |CITS: [24218293]|.</Description>
